1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,803
A desktop blogging client, such as Open Live Writer, can publish posts to

2
00:00:05,803 --> 00:00:07,444
an Orchard Core site.

3
00:00:07,444 --> 00:00:10,180
The XML-RPC module makes that possible.

4
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:15,590
This video covers enabling it, how a client finds your blog, and how you extend it.

5
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:22,876
The module provides two features.

6
00:00:22,876 --> 00:00:28,178
XML-RPC accepts a POST request at /xmlrpc, hands the method call to the

7
00:00:28,178 --> 00:00:32,309
registered handlers, and writes their response as XML.

8
00:00:32,309 --> 00:00:36,510
Remote Publishing adds the MetaWeblog API on top of it.

9
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:39,705
The Lists module answers the calls.

10
00:00:39,705 --> 00:00:43,284
To a client, a blog is a content item whose type has a

11
00:00:43,284 --> 00:00:47,340
List part, and its posts are the items that the list contains.

12
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:55,300
Under Tools, Features, search for Open Live Writer.

13
00:00:55,300 --> 00:01:00,037
Both features of the module match, in the Infrastructure category.

14
00:01:00,037 --> 00:01:03,720
Remote Publishing shows XML-RPC as its dependency.

15
00:01:04,173 --> 00:01:09,396
Let's enable Remote Publishing, and Orchard Core enables XML-RPC with it.

16
00:01:09,396 --> 00:01:14,024
The Lists and Media modules take part in the calls, and the Blog

17
00:01:14,024 --> 00:01:18,983
recipe enables both, with the Title, Markdown and Autoroute features.

18
00:01:23,196 --> 00:01:28,745
Two links in the head of a blog's page point a client to its details.

19
00:01:28,745 --> 00:01:34,886
The first, the Windows Live Writer manifest, is served at /xmlrpc/metaweblog.

20
00:01:35,351 --> 00:01:38,548
The manifest tells a client what the site supports.

21
00:01:38,548 --> 00:01:44,045
The supportsFileUpload element is Yes because the Media module declares it, and supportsSlug

22
00:01:44,045 --> 00:01:46,681
is Yes because the Autoroute module does.

23
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:50,790
The second link is the RSD document of the blog.

24
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:56,672
It names MetaWeblog as the preferred API, gives the blog's identifier, and points

25
00:01:56,672 --> 00:02:00,120
to /xmlrpc, where the client sends its calls.

26
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:07,241
The Lists module handles seven methods.

27
00:02:07,241 --> 00:02:11,461
They find the blogs a user can edit, read recent posts or a

28
00:02:11,461 --> 00:02:16,200
single post, create, edit and delete posts, and upload media files.

29
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:18,828
A call is an XML document.

30
00:02:18,828 --> 00:02:20,589
This one creates a post.

31
00:02:20,589 --> 00:02:24,287
It carries the method name, then the parameters in order:

32
00:02:24,287 --> 00:02:28,925
the blog identifier, the user name, the password, a struct with the post,

33
00:02:28,925 --> 00:02:30,510
and the publish flag.

34
00:02:31,260 --> 00:02:34,683
Each module reads and writes the members it knows.

35
00:02:34,683 --> 00:02:39,084
The Title module sets the display text of the item from the title.

36
00:02:39,084 --> 00:02:44,279
The Markdown and Html modules map the description, and Autoroute maps the slug,

37
00:02:44,279 --> 00:02:46,480
which is the path of the item.

38
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:50,207
Every call carries a user name and a password.

39
00:02:50,207 --> 00:02:52,383
The user also needs permissions:

40
00:02:52,383 --> 00:02:57,021
Edit content for others to list, read and save drafts, Publish or unpublish

41
00:02:57,021 --> 00:03:01,773
content for others to publish, Delete content for others to delete, and media

42
00:03:01,773 --> 00:03:03,720
permissions to upload files.

43
00:03:07,926 --> 00:03:11,091
In Content Items, here is a post that a client sent.

44
00:03:11,091 --> 00:03:15,184
The Lists module creates it with the first content type that the blog

45
00:03:15,184 --> 00:03:19,276
contains, BlogPost, and publishes it, because the call asks for that.

46
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:23,456
A call that does not ask to publish saves a draft, and so

47
00:03:23,456 --> 00:03:25,874
does a creation date in the future.

48
00:03:25,874 --> 00:03:29,000
Another call can edit the draft and publish it.

49
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,718
To map your own part, derive a driver from MetaWeblogDriver.

50
00:03:36,718 --> 00:03:40,769
BuildPost copies the part into the struct that goes to the client, and

51
00:03:40,769 --> 00:03:44,020
EditPost applies the struct that comes from the client.

52
00:03:44,770 --> 00:03:48,349
A driver can also declare capabilities for the manifest:

53
00:03:48,349 --> 00:03:50,946
the Autoroute driver sets supportsSlug.

54
00:03:50,946 --> 00:03:57,180
Register the driver as an IMetaWeblogDriver, in a startup that requires the Remote Publishing feature.

55
00:03:57,930 --> 00:04:01,655
To answer other methods, register an IXmlRpcHandler.

56
00:04:01,655 --> 00:04:05,895
ProcessAsync receives the method call and sets the response.

57
00:04:05,895 --> 00:04:10,776
An exception becomes a fault that carries its message, and a call that

58
00:04:10,776 --> 00:04:13,410
nothing answers returns status 500.

59
00:04:15,410 --> 00:04:17,347
That's the XML-RPC module:

60
00:04:17,347 --> 00:04:23,096
desktop clients publish to the blogs of your site, and drivers decide how each part maps.

61
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:24,670
Thanks for watching.

